X-Deja-AN: 144875590
sender: grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu
organization: University of Colorado
reply-to: grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.osf.osf1


A couple of people have asked about information on the memory channel.
I might point you to a paper in the Feb. '96 IEEE Micro about the
memory channel - the author was Rick Gillett, the technical director
(or some equiv. title) of the MC project. I've got a copy of this
paper in PDF format if people want that.

I've set up a mailing list

	memory-channel@cs.colorado.edu

for people using memory channel - I should publish this email address.
To subscribe, you should send email to majordomo@cs.colorado.edu with
the message "subscribe memory-channel".

The benefits we get from memory channel are:

	(1) faster TCP/IP between the machines
	(2) use of the native MC API for our distributed shared
	    memory system (we have three DSM protocols implemented
	    for simple applications & a simple message passing
	    library used by the DSM system)
	(3) Use of the MC for DEC-PVM for scientific computing
	    (this is still beta-test software)

We don't make use of ASE or the raw-disk protocol; thus, we're a
little unusual in our use of the MC. However, I know that others are
also working on MC native API message passing systems.

Dirk Grunwald                 Asst. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder



