newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <glL6sJWSMV1b9UGFQT@transarc.com>, Bill_von_Hagen@transarc.com writes...
>Amongst alot of cool hardware that a friend picked up for me, I found
>two 6-sided cartridges made by Digital, labelled RC25K-DC.  From the
>top, they look something like this:
> 
>            --------------------
>            |                  |
>            |                  |
>            |                  |
>            |                  |
>            |                  |
>            |                  |
>            \                  /
>             \                /
>              ----------------
> 
>(Sorry about the bad ASCII art!)  They have a small disk platter
>inside them, so they're clearly carts for some hard drive.  Could
>anyone give me any about these, and about what system(s) these drives
>were used on?  (If you have one and want to get rid of it, I'm
>interested!)


The RC25K-DC is a 26 Mbyte disk cartridge that goes in a RC25 drive.
In addition to the removable cartridge (the part you have), there was
also a fixed platter in the drive that was rotated by the same motor.
Whenever you spun the drive down to change removable cartridges, the
fixed platter had to be spun down too!  If the fixed platter was the
system disk (very often the case on the systems that had RC25's), the
system ground to a halt whenever you changed removable disks.

The RC25 was DEC's low-end non-floppy drive unit in the early 80's.  The
VAX 11/725 used them as a system disk and as a distribution media, 
and they could also be used on both Unibus and Q-bus PDP-11's.  You could
buy RT-11 distributions on RC25, in particular.

I believe the RC25 drive mechanism and cartridges were actually manufactured by
CDC.  The drive itself is quite fragile, as the heads can get knocked
out of alignment quite easily.  There's a notice at the front of the RC25
drive owner's manual telling you to be sure to put the shipping cartridge
in the drive when moving it anywhere - even to the other side of the room - 
to avoid damage to the unit.

New RC25K-DC cartridges are still available from DECdirect (1-800-DIGITAL).
The price is something like $325 each, if I remember right.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca) 



