There's a good article on WWW caching in the April issue of Digital
Age.  It says that Web caching is here--finally--to save your network
from congestion overload.  It says that your network can get clogged
up with the same WWW page, full of graphics and media, being downloaded
many times by different users.  Another scenario occurs when users try
to stop slow WWW pages from being downloaded, but the Web servers
don't know that that users have given up and keep flooding your network
with packets that are just going to be discarded.  

In some areas of the world direct Web browsing of URLs is not allowed
for these reasons.  The article says that a good introduction to
Web caching is at http://www.w3.org/pub/WWW/Proxies.

