In article <4j215f$3tt@news.scruz.net>, dthorpe@subliminal.com (Lurker) writes:
> horse1@ix.netcom.com(Horse ) wrote:
> 
>>I am considering purchasing a DEC Alpha system, which through my local
>>DEC reseller I can get at about 50% of list. If I am running the latest
>>version of MS NT, how well will the system run my old Windows 3.x
>>programs? I understand that DEC is now shipping a software
>>emulator/translator (FX!32) which essentially rewrites 32 bit programs
>>into Alpha native code. This is great for my 32 bit apps, but there are
>>a few which haven't been rewritten in 32 bit versions yet. I realize
>>that NT handles 16 bit with it's own x86 emulator, I'm just curious as
>>to how well. Some of my most important apps are not yet written in 32
>>bit.
> 
> Last I heard, FX!32 is still in limited beta.  It's not expected to
> ship until this summer.

At the Decus Canada symposium during the last week of February, we installed
FX!32 on a half-doze AXP systems.  At that time, the product was in Digital
internal field test, which means that it's not even generally available to
Digital employees. We were fortunate enough to be able to get the permission to
install FX!32 on systems that were available for general attendee use in our
Internet Cafe (which boasted 18 Alpha systems!).

I had limited experience with FX!32, but I'll give my brief impressions. 
Remember, this is based on the internal field test version, and it's likely to
change features and performance before its release mid-year.

The way FX!32 works is to run a server in the background.  You don't need to do
anything special prior to running your Intel application (unlike VEST, where
you first need to translate the executable).  When the program loader sees the
Intel code, it passes control to FX!32 which translate the executable if
required and keeps a bunch of information in its own database.  Every time you
run the same executable, FX!32 is watching, and optimizing its code.

We ran Netscape 2.0 for NT (Intel) on the Alpha systems.  It generally ran,
although Netscape did crash a few times (surprisingly very few).  There were
some Web pages it could never bring up.  Remember that FX!32 is still several
months away from release, so this problem will likely be fixed long before you
see it.

We also tried to run MS Powerpoint, but I heard it wasn't a pretty sight... 
The translation took an unreasonable amount of time.  The person who tried went
away for coffee (we were serving free capuccino and latte in our Internet Cafe,
thanks to TGV), came back and the translation was still going.  I don't know if
it ever completed.  Remember, this is still internal field test.

I seem to have the impression that FX!32 will be useful for those cases where
you have the odd Intel executable you need to run, but not for everyday use. 
If you really need to run Intel code, buy an Intel box.  This philosophy is in
line with I've been saying for years for products like SoftWindows, SoftPC,
A-Max, etc.

> In my experience, a 16 bit Win 3.1 app runs faster on a DEC
> AlphaStation 200 4/233 than on a Pentium 60.  Both running NT 3.51
> Workstation, with 32MB of RAM. 

I've heard that a Pentium 60 was rather a poor chip to be released, and that a
486/66 will outperform it.  If you're going to try to compare Pentium systems,
at least compare to a 120-133 Mhz system, since they're very popular now and
relatively inexpensive.

