I think you have a pre-conceived notion of what "good" is and aren't
analyzing the information available very well. You want to ascribe
"goodness" by metrics that mean very little for many applications.

Consider the bus bandwidth and memory constraints. We had a loaner SGI
powerchallenge system - one application was fairly highly tuned and
had very good processor utilization on 16 CPU's. However, tossing
another 16 CPU's at that application didn't help it at all (execution
time was reduced by about 4%, I recall).

In that problem, the memory system was the limiting bottleneck.

In the market that the 8400 is primarily targeted, databases and
buisness systems, memory bandwidth is very important. Although you
could build a back-plane to hold more CPU's (more likely, pack more on
a card), there's not always a benefit to doing that, either to the
application or from the perspective of market share.

Both the powerchallenge & 8400 are excellent high-end SMP systems, and
each is more appropriate for certain problem domains.

Btw, yes, you can do graphics on the 8400 using OpenGL.



