In article <4j6bma$jqb@cocoa.brown.edu> sgf@hydra.cfm.brown.edu (Sam Fulcomer) writes:
SF> The SGI numbers I was talking about were MPI latency. I don't know
SF> what the figures are for a native HIPPI API.... (currently
SF> sub-100?...)

SF> I'd guess DEC's MPI (yeah, MPI _is_ important, DEC...) numbers
SF> ought to achieve sub-10 usec for 1-byte messages... (assuming
SF> reality on the 4-usec native...).
---

We have a memory channel between two AlphaServer 2100's. The following
comments apply to our field-test hardware & configuration. I would
expect things to improve, but they're already very impressive.

The latency using the native MemoryChannel API is about
3.5microseconds - but that's a fairly deceptive number, because the
situation is much better than the 3.5ms makes it seem.

There is very little overhead on "sending" a message. For example,
using the native API, "sending" is simply executing a STORE
instruction. Likewise, receiving is simply executing a LOAD
instruction (or doing nothing - the data just shows up). You don't
need interrupts, so the load on the processor can be quite low.

Since many people won't want to code to the native API (which looks a
lot like shared memory segments), the PVM and MPI times are more
important for many people.

I don't know about MPI, but the PVM times they have discussed
publically (@ SuperComputing '95) are ~15microseconds, user-space to
user-space. We've seen similar numbers in the past, but haven't
benchmarked it lately (we're too busy using it). I would expect the
MPI numbers would be slightly better if you use the messaging types
that let you specify the buffer location in advance.

On thing to recall when comparing thos numbers to e.g., the SGI HIPPI
and pending SuperHIPPI numbers is that the MemoryChannel numbers are
for *secure*, *shared* communication. That is, multiple users can be
using the channel at the same time - the device doesn't get mapped
into user memory, exposing security problems.

Personally, I hope the MemoryChannel, and similar networks such as the
Shrimp network at Princeton and the proposed(?) Hamlyn at HP are the
network hardware standards of the future. They cleanly handle security
by pushing the security overhead to the setup phase and using the
underlying TLB/memory management structure to handle protection
following the setup.

We're also working on a device driver for the Myrinet hardware
(nominal 640 Mbit/second bidirection, or 1.2GBit total bandwidth).
I'll be interesting to compare the two networks. Myrinet has been
reported to have 14microsecond latency for the Illinois "Fast
Messages" protocol, but that requires mapping the device into user
memory (not acceptable, in my book).




