Dan Moore <pergamon@iasmh.bsu.edu> wrote:
>Hello.
>
>I'm looking for information on the VTxxx series of terminals.  I have a 
>VT240 currently, but it's a little slow for me.  What VTxxx actually 
>communicate at 19.2k or above?  Also, are there any other VTxxx that are 
>color? 

The VT340 is color.  It also has 2 separate comm ports that allow you to 
run 2 sessions at the same time (or you can run 2 sessions on the same 
com port if you are connectd to equipment that knows how to support 
multisessions).  Both ports can be set to 19.2K.


> I also have the opportunity to get a DEC rainbow for about $20.00, 
>but I can't find _any_ info on them.  Could I use one of these as 
>a dumb terminal?  If so, would it communicate at 19.2k or above? 

I have a Rainbow 100A.  It allows you to select a baud rate in setup up 
to 19.2K.  I think there were com programs that would set the uart 
directly for speeds higher than 19.2, but I'm not sure the system could 
really keep up.  The Rainbow had a build in terminal emulator that 
emulated a VT100.  You could also boot either MS/DOS or CMP 86/80 and run 
a terminal emulator.  Some of the emulators supported REGIS graphics, 
others didn't.


> Lastly, does anyone know anything about the DEC VAX 6000-210 such as >speed (relative to something current) or availability of par=
ts/features? > Is $200.00 a decent price for one?
>

The 6000-210 (or 6210 as it was called then) was rated at about 2.8 VUPs.
A VAX 6000-610 is rated at about 32 VUPs.  If this is a complete system, 
then $200 is an excelent price.  If it is too slow for your needs, the 
system can be upgraded by adding processors or changing to a faster 
processor (this will take $$$).  Upgrading to a 6000-510 or 6000-610 
would probably require a power supply upgrade or addition of regulator 
cards (they require a voltage not provided by the original power 
supplies).  Parts are available for the system, though they will not be 
cheap compared to the $200 for the system. The system uses the same XMI 
bus as the newer VAX 6000 systems.


