In article <4iq8ql$jdd@itchy.itsnet.com>, thatcher@twiki.spire.com (Bruce F. Thatcher) writes:

[me]>>I [...] tried a promotional copy of WordPerfect for VMS recently. 
[me]>>[...] I rapidly formed the opinion that it was unusable. 

[Bruce]>I'm curious as to _why_ you decided it was unusable.  
[Bruce]>Was it that you are not familiar with the standard WP function-key 
[Bruce]>operation?  

A fair question. No, I'm not very familiar with WordPerfect in any form. I can
just about busk my way through a simple document on a PC running the version 5
flavour. I can't remember key strokes and sequences for particular operations -
I've got a scrap of paper pinned up on my board which tells me how to get
PostScript out of a Wp file for instance. DECwrite does that very easily.

I think it's a bit like the difference between emacs and EVE. I keep telling
people that all you really have to remember with EVE is where the DO key is.
DECwrite has the help under the fingers, WP is (IMHO) a steep learning curve. 
That doesn't change the fact that 99.9% of the people in this university use
WordPerfect (or Word) and 0.0001% use DECwrite (me).

[Bruce]>Did it consume a lot of system resources?  Did you have difficulty
[Bruce]>installing the product? 

No and no. It took a bit of time de-installing it and throwing away the HELP
entries and queues that the installation created.

I'm not trying to attack the product, I speak as I find. It's not for me.
However, my wife has WP6.1 on her Windows 95 PC at home and she and the kids
love it. I've played a little and it does seem a lot closer to the
functionality that one needs.

	Cheers,
	Andy

