In article <4ijbim$lng@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk> rf@cl.cam.ac.uk (Robin Fairbairns) writes:


   What my brain was saying was "I know of no _chip_ that couldn't run
   VMS".  What my fingers said is another matter.  My apologies to anyone
   who was confused.

There are modified Alpha chips coming down the DEC pipe that will run
neither VMS nor Digital Unix. That would have limited them to lesser
operating systems such as Windows NT, if it wasn't for Linux which will
run even on a lobotomized CPUs.

I don't fully understand what exactly is disabled on the chip, but I
think it has to do with inability to map all regions of virtual address
space to physical address: Windows NT can't use them anyway because
it is a 32-bit system, and 64-bit Linux works around that. Come to think about
