TommyLee Whitlock (tommy@intercon.com) wrote:
: I need some info on how to remap illegal characters on a VAX running OpenVMS.  

: Our NFS client writes Apple Double files, the resource fork of which shows up 
: with a %.  I've heard that all VMS-type NFS servers can remap the % on the 
: fly, but I'd like to know how this is done.  Can anyone answer this or point 
: me in the right direction (I do not have access to a VAX)?  I'm looking 
: specifically for info with UCX 3.3

I believe % is a valid character in the POSIX file system.  UCX can export
a POSIX file system, and the POSIX kit will allow you to access the file
from VMS.  You can also access the file outside of POSIX if you work out the
inode mapping.

And if you do use the POSIX file system, read carefully the instructions for
restoring a single file from a VMS BACKUP of such.  Unless you have the
required disk space for that operation I'd save those file systems with tar.

Or you could pick up a different IP product, which translates all non-VMS
file names according to a translation table.

