I'm certainly the guy named John that Chuck mentions in the history referred 
to above. Chuck and I shared an office (cubicle) on the second floor of 
building 12 when Chuck first joined DEC. At the time Roger Pyle and I were 
the PDP-8 programming group. Chuck made it three. Several months prior to 
Chuck's arrival, I had just finished re-writing the Dectape service routine 
in Decsys-7 (on the PDP-7) and Roger Pyle (who was an old Decsys-7 hand at a 
customer site in San Diego) was putting together a new PDP-8 group from 
scratch. He was obviously impressed enough by my -7 work to make me his first 
employee. The group was put together to write some operating software to be 
based around the DF-32, a 32kb (yes, kilobytes) fixed head disk, the first 
disk hardware entirely built by DEC. Prior to this there was no operating 
software for the -8, programs were just loaded and run standalone. Roger and 
I designed and built the Disk Monitor System. I designed the disk structure 
to mirror Dectape, with 201 (octal) words per block. The extra word was a 
link pointer to the next block of the file. This allowed me to debug the code 
before the DF-32 was ready by substituting a Dectape driver for the disk 
driver. Slow, but it worked. 

Shortly after Chuck's arrival I went off to join Don Witcraft in building 
TSS-8, "the world's first commercial minicomputer timesharing system". Chuck 
assumed responsibility for the Disk Monitor System. Roger went off to built 
typesetting systems, and Chuck took over PDP-8 development. By the time Stan 
Rabinowitz (who started this thread) arrived I had leveraged what I'd learned 
about timesharing into a job in the PDP-10 monitor group.

-- 
jeverett@wwa.com (John V. Everett)




