Scott Weinstein wrote:
> 
> I've just bought a used VS3100 and I'm very eager to install VMS on it.
> 
> I have a few questions - the machine didn't come with any
> documentation. I've been searching through only INFO-VAX archives and
> have looked at the microvax homepage --  which is very helpful.
> 
> 1) which model is it? I think it might be a vs3100m38, but I'm not sure.
> The nameplate says
> VAXStation 3100, and on the back it says VS42A-
> 
> 2) Is it in good working condition?
> 
> I can get both the monitor and the serial/printer Console to work.
> 
> On bootup I get
> 
> KA42-A  V1.3
> 
> F...E...D...C...B...A...9...8...7...6...5...4_..3_..2_..1?..

Underscore means that a test was skipped.  Usually because the hardware 
is not present.  Question mark means that the test failed.

>  ?  C  0080  0000.4001
> ??  1  00C0  0011.700E
> 
> What do the underscores and question marks mean?

Question marks indicate errors.  A single question mark is a non-fatal 
error and a double mark is a fatal error.  A fatal error will prevent the 
system from autobooting.

I assume that this test was run from a terminal connected to the console 
port.  The "C" error is normal in this case.  I think that it's a warning 
saying that the console port could not be tested (since you are already 
using it).

The "1" error probably means that you didn't have a terminator on your 
Ethernet port.  You have both an AUI and thinwire port on the back of the 
machine.  There is a switch between them which is used to select the 
port.  Select the port you want to use and make sure it has a terminator 
on it.  Then rerun the tests.

> if I do a test 50 I get
> 
> >> test 50
> KA42-A  V1.3          ^D^B^A
> ID 08-00-2B-11-28-CF
> 
>    MONO     0000.0001
>    CLK      0000.0001
>    NVR      0000.0001
>  ? DZ       0000.4001
>       00000001 00000001 00000001 00004001 00000001 00000000
>    MEM      0008.0001
>       00800000
>    MM       0000.0001
>    FP       0000.0001
>    IT       0000.0001
>    STRG-1   7777.0001  V1.30
>       00000000 00000000 FFFFFFFF 00000000
>    SCSI-A   0808.0001  V1.30
>       FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 00000001 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF03 FFFFFF05
>    SYS      0000.0001
> ?? NI       0011.700E
> 
> Is this healthy?

The "?" by DZ is the TEST C results and NI is TEST 1.

Unless someone has done some surgery to the motherboard, you have a 
VS3100/30, not a model 38. You have the SCSI/ST506 disk controller with 
an RX23 drive connected to the ST506 side.  You have a single SCSI bus 
which is shared between internal and external devices. You do not have a 
color graphics card.

> >>> sh dev
>  VMS/VMB  ULTRIX    ADDR    DEVTYP  NUMBYTES  RM/FX  WP  DEVNAM  REV
>  -------  ------  --------  ------  --------  -----  --  ------  ---
>  ESA0     SE0     08-00-2B-11-28-CF
> 
>  DUA2     RX2               DISK               RM        RX23
> 
>  DKA300   RZ3     A/3/0/00  DISK     52.4 MB   FX        RZ22
>  ...HostID....    A/6       INITR
> 
> 3) As I understand, this machine should have no problem with most SCSI
> disks and CDs. Is the bus SCSI I or SCSI II?

SCSI II devices work fine with the bus.

> There seems to be a real problem with adding SCSI devices, the
> internal connector is like nothing I've ever seen. It's a very wide and
> compact loop cable with 2 connectors.

I think that the SCSI connectors are standard 50 pin headers.  They 
should just plug into any SCSI drive.

> The external SCSI connector is a 68 pin male compact scsi connector.
> Every scsi device I own is 50 pin. Are there adaptor cables I can buy?
> Where do I get them?

You need a VS3100 external SCSI cable. I don't know the part number.

> The ideal situation would be to find a way to put in a 500 meg disk
> inside the case, this way I could get rid of the slow, small and loud
> rz22 drive. I don't know how to do this with the odd internal cable...

As I said, you should be able to use any single ended (non-differential) 
SCSI drive with this system. The only restriction is that your boot drive 
must not be larger than 1GB.

> 4) It seems that this machine has 8 megs of ram. Is this enough to
> run VMS? I don't plan on running a windowing system, but I do want to
> play with DEC C. How much does VS ram cost?

Well, VMS is a virtual memory system, so you can get it to boot.  If you 
try to do too much with it, however, you may just get the system 
thrashing.  Your memory needs are going to depend on what you do with the 
system.  For a single user terminal based system, 8MB may be enough.  
(Depending on the applications you run, of course.) If you want to run 
Motif, I would recommend at least 24 MB. 

Good luck!

