I'm not a hardware expert by a long shot; but I do have a VS3100-30 and
a hardware manual for a VS3100-40.

The underscores in the powerup test readout indicate that portion of
the powerup test didn't pass. The "4" is for the optional graphics card.
Your machine probably doesn't have one. "3" and "2" are reserved for later 
use and can be ignored.

The test identifiers are:
F Base video
E System clock
D Nonvolatile ram
C Serial line controller
B Memory
A Memory-management unit
9 Floating point unit
8 Interval timer
7 Disk controller
6 Disk controller
5 Interrupt controller & Ethernet ID ROM
4 Optional 8-plane graphics coprocessor
3 Reserved for later use
2 Reserved for later use
1 Ethernet network interconnect


>
>
> ?  C  0080  0000.4001
>??  1  00C0  0011.700E


The "?  C..." indicates something questionable with the serial line controller.
It's not a "hard" fault. Do you maybe have something plugged into the printer
port? 

The "?? 1..." means there is no ethernet cable or terminator. The double
question marks indicated this is a hard fault. Get the "T" connector and a
pair of terminators for the thin-wire port and that should fix it.

>
>if I do a test 50 I get
>
>>> test 50
>KA42-A  V1.3          ^D^B^A
>ID 08-00-2B-11-28-CF
>
>   MONO     0000.0001
>   CLK      0000.0001
>   NVR      0000.0001
> ? DZ       0000.4001
>      00000001 00000001 00000001 00004001 00000001 00000000

OK this is a status breakdown for various tests of the serial line controller.
The statuses from left to right are keyboard port, video, communication port,
printer port, keyboard selftest, and mouse selftest.

Mouse selftest? 

Hm... Well that's what the manual says anyway 8-).


>   MEM      0008.0001
>      00800000
>   MM       0000.0001
>   FP       0000.0001
>   IT       0000.0001
>   STRG-1   7777.0001  V1.30
>      00000000 00000000 FFFFFFFF 00000000
>   SCSI-A   0808.0001  V1.30
>      FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 00000001 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF03 FFFFFF05

Each one of these corresponds to a SCSI ID. "FFFFFF05" indicates the ID is
unused. ID #7 ("FFFFFF03") is the SCSI controller. ID #8 is reserver for
devices requiring the highest priority on the bus.

I don't have a complete list of what all of the possible values mean; but
I do have the following which were examples in the manual:
FFFFFF05 Open
FFFFFF03 Controller
00000001 RZ23
05020001 RRD40 (CD player)
01000000 TK50 tape drive

>   SYS      0000.0001
>?? NI       0011.700E             
>

The mnemonics list for this test is:
CLK   System clock
8PLN  graphics card
DZ    Serial line controller
FP    floating point unit
SCSIA SCSI-a controller
SCSIB SCSI-b controller
IT    Interval timer
MEM   memory
MM    memory management unit
MONO  Monochrome video circuits on system module
NI    Network interconnect
NVR   Nonvolatile ram
SYS   Interrupt controller & Ethernet ID ROM

I do not know what an STRG-1 is. It looks like a non-SCSI disk controller.
My machine has two SCSI's. Your's apparently has a  SCSI and something
else.

