In article <SWEIN.96Mar27104412@eve.albany.edu>, swein@csc.albany.edu (Scott Weinstein) writes:
> 1) which model is it? I think it might be a vs3100m38, but I'm not sure.
> The nameplate says
> VAXStation 3100, and on the back it says VS42A-

  If the cover matches the innards, it's a VS3100 model 30 (assuming it's
in the short box).

> 2) Is it in good working condition?
> 
> I can get both the monitor and the serial/printer Console to work.
> 
> On bootup I get
> 
> KA42-A  V1.3
> 
> F...E...D...C...B...A...9...8...7...6...5...4_..3_..2_..1?..
> 
> 
>  ?  C  0080  0000.4001
> ??  1  00C0  0011.700E
> 
> What do the underscores and question marks mean?

  The underscores mean the option isn't installed. 4 is the optional multi-
plane color framebuffer (like a WS01), 3 and 2 were never implemented.

  1? is the Ethernet controller - you don't have an Ethernet hooked up or
the switch for thin/AUI is set wrong. C is the pseudo-DZ11 (console and
printer ports). It always acts like this.

>>>> sh dev
>  VMS/VMB  ULTRIX    ADDR    DEVTYP  NUMBYTES  RM/FX  WP  DEVNAM  REV
>  -------  ------  --------  ------  --------  -----  --  ------  ---
>  ESA0     SE0     08-00-2B-11-28-CF
> 
>  DUA2     RX2               DISK               RM        RX23
> 
>  DKA300   RZ3     A/3/0/00  DISK     52.4 MB   FX        RZ22
>  ...HostID....    A/6       INITR
> 
> 3) As I understand, this machine should have no problem with most SCSI
> disks and CDs. Is the bus SCSI I or SCSI II?

  Looks like you have an older system with the ST506/SCSI controller.

  These systems only do async (1.5MB/sec) SCSI. Just about anything should
work (slowly) in there. 5400 or 7200 RPM sync/fast drives are probably 
wasted in there, though.  

> There seems to be a real problem with adding SCSI devices, the
> internal connector is like nothing I've ever seen. It's a very wide and
> compact loop cable with 2 connectors. 

  That's standard for these. The terminators are on the controller card,
and the cable starts and finishes up there. The 50-pin connectors are
standard - just make sure termination is off on any drives you attach
internally.

  To mount drives, depending on the style of case you have, you should be
able to get a box of assorted grommets at Radio Shack. The next-to-the-
smallest size will fit in the slots in the case - use 6/32 x 1/4" machine
screws to attach the drive to the grommets.

> The external SCSI connector is a 68 pin male compact scsi connector.
> Every scsi device I own is 50 pin. Are there adaptor cables I can buy?
> Where do I get them?

  From DEC. You'll want BC56H-0x, where x is 3, 6, or 9 for 3, 6, or 9
foot cables. Note that just because this looks like the wrong gender of
a wide SCSI connector doesn't mean it is - it's one of those DEC propri-
etary things.

> The ideal situation would be to find a way to put in a 500 meg disk
> inside the case, this way I could get rid of the slow, small and loud
> rz22 drive. I don't know how to do this with the odd internal cable...

  Just unplug the RZ22 and put the new disk in, using the same connector.

> 4) It seems that this machine has 8 megs of ram. Is this enough to
> run VMS? I don't plan on running a windowing system, but I do want to
> play with DEC C. How much does VS ram cost?

  Yes, it'll work (slowly). However, the RZ22 isn't big enough.

