x-mailer: Mozilla 1.1N (Windows; I; 16bit)

compac I can be written in tk50 and tz30 tape drives only. Capacity is 
100 meg. Compac I tapes can be read in TZ30, Tk50, TK70, TK85, TK86, TK87

compac II can be written by tk70 tape drives only. If the tape has not 
been initialized before it can be writen in TK50 compatible mode. If the 
tape is writen in TK70 mode them you can not read it in a TK50 or TZ30 
drive. Unsure of capacity. Compac II tapes can be read in TK70, TK85, 
Tk86, TK87

compac III can be written in a TK85 TK86 or TK87 tape drive the capacity 
is dependent in the drive. TK87 writes 20 GB in compressed mode. TK8* can 
read Compac I, II and III tape cartridges.

Compac IV is now available for the new TK88 tape drives 40 GB capacity.



Initialization problems. 

When a DLT tape is first initialized timing tracks are written on the 
tape. Each time that the tape is physically loaded in a drive the drive 
uses those timing tracks to align the heads and to set the density mode. 
If the tape has been initialized at a capacity the drive doesn't 
understand the drive lights will flash and you will be unable to mount 
the drive. The only way to "clear" the initialization timing tracks is to 
Bulk Erase (degauss) the tape cartridge.


Hope this helps

Brian V





