Nader Behzad <nbehzad@ee.ualberta.ca> wrote:

>Does anyone have gcc2.6.0 compiled for DEC? Could you tell if there
>are patches available for it. I have a hard time compiling this version
>of gcc on my machine. It complains about running out of bounds. There is
>a patch available for gcc2.7.2 but nothing for gcc2.6.0.
>
>I get floating exception problems when my source is compiled with gcc2.7.2.
>This code has been working on every other platform (using gcc2.6.0).


Dear Nader,

  I use g++2.7.2 on the alphas.  At first, when we did a port
from SunOS to Digital Unix of the code, we had many
floating exceptions which never showed up before.

  In each case, it turned out we were:

  1) assigning invalid data (uninitialized memory) to a variable (even if we
	never used the variable again and didn't care about he value) 
or
  2) doing a division by zero (which SunOS would just ignore)

In both cases, we'd rather have the computer crash and tell us
we were doing something wrong (it is wrong, even though we had
never noticed the problem before) than have it go on as if
nothing had happened, as would happen in SunOS.

  It's possible that your program is doing such things, which
other architectures allow but which alphas don't, but which
are nevertheless wrong.

  If that's the case, it is your program which needs correction,
not gcc.  Though your situation may not be the same as ours
was.

  I hope your problem gets solved one way or the other.  

Regards,

PeterM

 



