>                 ASC
> __________________________________________________________________
> CARISSIMI, Alexandre                  Alexandre.Carissimi@imag.fr
> Laboratoire de Modelisation et Calcul - LMC
> Institut National Polytechnique de Grenoble - INPG
> 100, Rue des Mathematiques                  Fone: (33) 76.51.46.03
> 38041 - Grenoble - Cedex 9  FRANCE          Fax : (33) 76.63.12.63
> 
>  "Life has more imagination than our dreams" C.Colombus - 1492
> __________________________________________________________________

Prior to Digital UNIX 4.0 (which will be shipping very soon), the
threads package (DECthreads) implements the POSIX 1003.4a/D4 draft
interface. This is also known as "DCE threads", because the thread
package shipped with OSF DCE was based on the DECthreads code base,
and has the same interface. The scheduling model is "one to one"
mapping on Mach kernel threads.

For Digital UNIX 4.0 (the first version to ship since 1003.1c-1995
was approved last June), we have re-architected DECthreads using the
final POSIX 1003.1c-1995 programming interface. We have also used a
"many to few" scheduling model with kernel upcalls -- in other words,
when a thread blocks in the user level library, for example, to block
on a mutex or condition variable, DECthreads simply moves another
"user thread" onto the "kernel thread" without involving the kernel.
When the kernel blocks a thread, for example, to wait for an I/O, it
notifies the DECthreads user-level scheduler so that we can dust off
an inactive user thread and make use of the available execution slot.

