In article <4ingbh$7ud@nic.umass.edu>, Tom Boldway <jboldway@student.umass.edu> writes:
>I've connected a vt220 to my 'new' microvax, and it powers up. It's got 
>microvms4.2, but it asks for: "username: and password:." I've discovered 
>a way to log in and see the root account and type things such as 
>"userlog," which show the usernames, but where are the corresponding 
>passwords?
>
>Anybody have a clue as to how I can use this thing?
>
>please EMAIL at jboldway@student.umass.edu
>
>THANKS!
>
>P.S. it has 3 megs of mem and a rd-52, ka630
>

VMS does not store passwords. It only stores hashed passwords. So if
you lose a password the only thing you can do is change it. There is
nothing to read to find out what it was.

The VMS FAQ covers the topic "I lost the SYSTEM password - what do I do?"
in pretty good detail. One copy of it is at 
http://www.hhs.dk/vms/txt/vmsfaq.txt .

What the various methods amount to is simply changing the SYSTEM password
to something you know. It's pretty simple to do, but check the FAQ for the
recommended method for a uVAX. I have a VAXStation, and I think this is 
handled the same way on a uVAX; but I wouldn't want to steer you wrong.

--
David Bratton
University of Houston System
DBratton@uh.edu



