In article <827274832.26168@globe.demon.co.uk>, Tim Gordon <tim@globe.demon.co.uk> writes:
|Can anyone give me an answer to the following:
|
|What are the limitations of LAT when used on a bridged network?
|
|I am told that LAT can only be used sucessfully on a LAN if 
|there are not too many bridge hops. For example we are 
|currently using LAT to connect some terminal servers to a host 
|via 3 bridges and a killostream circuit.
|
|This sometimes gives timeout problems and I need to know what 
|the technical limitations are for LAT so that I can rule this 
|out as a cause.

Well, LAT *was* designed to be used only on a Local Area Network -
hence the first two letters of its name.

According to DEC, there is a limit of 8 bridges in a path.  I don't
believe there's any firm technical reason for this (unless it
would be architectural constraints on spanning tree.)  I've successfully
run LAT on a network with at least 8 bridge hops in the path, including
bridged T1 links.

LAT does have a fixed 1 second retransmission timer.  Therefore
a network over which you're running LAT should have a round
trip time consistently well under 1 second.  Bridges and Ethernets 
will not bump you up against this limit, but if you're bridging over
a satellite link, then you may be in trouble.

I don't know what a "killostream" circuit is.  If it's something
like a leased line, then I wouldn't worry about it if it delivered
at least 500 or so Kbps.  If it were under 100Kbps, I'd be 
concerned, and if it were under about 30Kbps, I wouldn't bother
trying to run LAT.  LAT can NOT cope with congestion - if a 
slow serial line should get congested, such that due to queueing
delays the RTT thru it should grow to over 1 second, then bye-bye 
LAT.

The usual cause of LAT disconnects is having the retransmission
timers set too low.  By default, they tend to be set to something
like 8 or 20 seconds (retransmits).  Such timer settings are quite
inadequate on a complex bridged network where you might have
spanning tree flaps or boxes rebooting or whatnot.

I recommend always boosting the LAT timers to their maxima -
typically 120 - 255 seconds.  This needs to be done on everyone
talking LAT - your hosts, your terminal servers and your PCs.
(On VMS, the relevant LATCP parameters are KEEPALIVE_TIMER
(max 255) and RETRANSMIT_TIMER (max 120).

Cheers,

Aaron

