In <4icsfe$7la@insosf1.netins.net> "Wayne A. Andersen" <WANDERSEN@NETINS.INS> writes:

>We have 10 VRT19-HA,H4 monitors purchased in 1993 with 
>Vaxstations 4000s.  They were made by Sony and have three video 
>inputs.

>When we bought these system, I noticed in the DEC catalog that 
>they sold an identical monitor for use with PCs.

>Does anyone have any ideas on how these monitors can be converted 
>for use with PCs?  If I can track down the model number of the PC 
>version of these monitors, maybe DEC can still supply the boards 
>that are different.

Wayne,

you can use these monitors on a PC, but (as usual there is always a "but"). :-)

1. You need a special cable with some TTL-logic.
Reason: The VRT-monitors are using RGB-input with syncronisation on green.
So you have to mix your VGA-output signals to conform this input model.
2. You probably need a programmable video card.
I'm just typing this text on a PC looking on a VR299 monitor. 
The system video card is a (german) ELSA winner 1000 TRIO card (S3)
with this parameters
Resolution 1152x864 with 256 colours
58.8 Hz picture frequency
52.92 kHz line frequency and
78.752 MHz pixel frequency.
Works fine and reliable.

Regards

Andreas

