In article <4ig9ea$jsc@dfw-ixnews3.ix.netcom.com>,
    		melaughp@ix.netcom.com(Peter Melaugh ) writes:
> I need help/suggestions....
>
> Here's the situation:  we just installed a "gigaswitch" on a Vax 4710
> (running a Mumps based lab system).  What we used to observe under the
> previous DSSI disk setup was:

[snip]

>     5.  "user's perceived response time" - consistantly good
>
> What we have now with the "gigaswitch" (basically fiber run from the
> Vax 4710 to a separate Alpha 1000 is:   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

[snip]

>     5.  "user's perceived response time - very poor (slow, long waits,
> etc.)
>
> What is going on?  This is DSM under VMS on a Vax 4710.  We expected the disk
                                                              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> throughput to get better, and it probably is -- but it seems like the online
  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

        I think your expectations were unfounded...

> user's priorities are all screwed up or something.  You know anyone who might
> have some experience with the "gigaswitch" in an Vax environment?  It's
> tempting to go back to the slow DSSI setup.

        That may be the right  choice.   You clearly have an expectation
    of  the the Gigaswitch being "fast" and the DSSI being "slow".   For
    disk access, that's simply not the case.  As you stated above, using
    the Gigaswitch is basically doing  a  "fiber  run".   Throughput  on
    fiber  for  disk access is NOT as good as throughput over DSSI.  The
    hierarchy is,

         Best  <-----------------> Worst

        CI > DSSI > SCSI > FDDI > Ethernet

    There is not only  the  media  bandwidth  to  be considered, but CPU
    overhead  in  formatting data to go over the particular link,  _and_
    issues of contention/collisions.  CI is still the best, and will  be
    for  some  time  to  come.   DSSI comes next and is better than SCSI
    primarily because of  "improvements"  over  the  SCSI  protocol that
    Digital  implemented in DSSI.  SCSI takes more CPU cycles to  handle
    even though bandwidth numbers might make you  think  it  was  faster
    than DSSI.  (This could change in the future if SCSI gets faster and
    if  DSSI  stays  the  same.)  FDDI  is  slower  than  SCSI and takes
    _substantial_ CPU to interact  with  the device.  Ethernet, the same
    but slower.

        So in spite of the Gigaswitch itself having impressive bandwidth
    numbers, the bottle neck is  most  likely  the FDDI interface to the
    VAX 4710.

> BTW, this wasn't a clustered environment before we installed the gigaswitch
> -- with the gigaswitch we basically cluster the Vax 4710 with the Alpha 1000
> running the gigaswitch.

        While being clustered could have  an  impact, I doubt that's the
    main  issue.   What  you  may  want to  consider  in  order  to  get
    performance better than DSSI is to move to CI.  I don't know  enough
    about a VAX 4710 to say whether that's a possibility for you in this
    case or not.  But if Digital doesn't offer a CI adapter for the VAX,
    some  3rd party vendor may (MTI, CMD?).  Similarly, I don't know the
    CI issues relating to your Alpha 1000, but there is now a CI adapter
    for PCI, so maybe...

        If you can get CI adapters  for  the 4710 and the 1000, then you
    can  go  to any of several StorageWorks solutions that have  the  CI
    controller in the StorageWorks cabinet (rather than  going  with  an
    HSC).  That would give you the best throughput plus controller-based
    mirroring  (no  more host-based shadow sets, thank you :-).  Another
    option would be to go the  HSC  route  but buy used HSC's (they're a
    lot  cheaper  :-), but this would depend on a thorough  analysis  of
    your I/O needs versus the capabilities of the HSC.

        I should add a caveat: I have  no feeling whatsoever for the I/O
    capabilities  of  a VAX 4710.  It could be the 4710 is  "maxed  out"
    just by DSSI, in which case trying to do CI wouldn't help.   Try  to
    find out what your various options are as well as the limitations of
    the 4710's I/O system.

        -Ken
