In article <4hn1vo$4gr@mozo.cc.purdue.edu>,
mckay@net-kitchen.com (Dwight McKay) writes:
> I'm trying to setup a VS3100/76 with a 4Gb fujitsu disk as the system disk.
> VMS installation proceeds fine through the standalone backup phase having
> booted and copied VMS from CDROM. However, the machine will not boot from the
> disk after backup finishes.
> 
> I have checked the disk by taking it to a MicroVAX II with a CMD SCSI
> controller in it and it will boot successfully there.
> 
> Is there a problem with SCSI on the VS3100/76? What's the solution?

It is my understanding that the 3100 controlware can't boot address
more than 1G "deep" into a disk.  Because the boot areas
of OVMS tend to be deep into the alphabet (SYS$COMMON, VMS$COMMON),
disks in excess of 1G usually end of with the boot areas too deep
for the processor controlware to reach.

The solution is to either force the boot areas into low blocks
of the disk, or use a small system disk, and the big disk
as a non-system disk.

Digital says that there are no plans to upgrade the 3100 controlware.

Given when they last manufactured a VS3100/30 or VS3100/76, that is
not a big surprise.





