Chris Scheers  <asi@airmail.net> wrote:
>I have heard that the difference between an AXP system which can support 
>VMS and one which only supports NT is in the memory management hardware. 
>If the extra hardware required for VMS is not present, VMS won't run on 
>it.

This is wrong.  The memory management hardware is all on-chip, and
there aren't differences between the various chips in that respect (at
least within series -- I know that the 211* chips differ from the 210*
chips).

>Assuming the above is true, how does Digital UNIX fit into the picture?  
>If a system can run Digital UNIX, can it also run VMS?  Or is there 
>another variant which comes into play?

Digital Unix only uses 2 of the 4 protection modes that the hardware
supplies (as presumably does N[o]T[hankyou]).

>This all leads up to: I have heard that Digital UNIX will run on a 
>Multia.  Will VMS?

I believe not, though I've never had access to a Multia to check.  The
issue is not the processor hardware but the device drivers.  There are
certainly issues about the availability of drivers for some machines
(a critical one being the display device, which IIRC was the issue
here).
--
