  The LK401 (LK201, and varients) are DEC unique serial devices that
  are only useful on VAXstations, Alpha DEC 3000 workstations, and
  terminals older than the VT500.

  New Alpha workstations and new terminals are using PS2 compatable
  keyboards.

  Because the PS2 101 and 102-key keyboards are difficult at best to
  use on OpenVMS (and Digital Unix for that matter), there are
  several keyboards that look like the LK401, and can be made to
  function identically to the LK401.

  The LK250, and it's follow on the LK450 were designed for the PC
  for users wanting LK400 keyboard functionality.  The LK450 has
  both the LK400 as well as the PS2 *and* the WPS gold key engravings.
  The LK450 can operate in multiple modes to allow each set of
  engravings to operate when connected to a PC (with the proper
  keyboard driver installed - which comes with the KB).

  The LK411 was designed as a true LK401 "replacement" for systems
  with the PS2 connector.  It was not really designed for use on a PC.

  You may plug the LK450 or the LK411 into any OpenVMS or Digital
  Unix workstation with a PS2 keyboard connector, and it will
  automatically work as a LK401 replacement.

