X-Deja-AN: 142714695
references: <4hpf7v$6j8@enterprise.netserv.chula.ac.th>
organization: Amherst College, Amherst MA, USA
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

Montree Suntichaikul (montree@chula.edu) wrote:
: We installed the C2 security feature combined with NIS for our
: Alpha2100 systems. We found that the error message like once a user
: gets login to his accounts:

: Can't rewrite to protected password entry for user xxxxxxx

You don't want to run NIS and C2 on the same system if you have a lot
of users.  The problem is that C2 keeps a lot of information on each
user, last login and so forth, that must be updated everytime the user
logs on.  With C2 alone this isn't a problem because each user has
their own file in /tcb/files/auth/...  The problem is that NIS with C2
moves all this info to a single protected password database.  This
file must be written to by each login.  This creates a conflict for
control of the file.  If you have a lot of users, it may take several
attempts before an individual actually gets control of the file so the
login can complete.  And since people learn to retry when they fail
the first time, a backlog of people trying to login builds up making
the problem even worse. I learned this the hard way.

--

Paul Chapin
Unix Systems Manager
Amherst College




