In article <4i08jc$8b9@news-f.iadfw.net>, Chris Scheers <asi@airmail.net> writes:
|>I have heard that the difference between an AXP system which can support 
|>VMS and one which only supports NT is in the memory management hardware. 
|>If the extra hardware required for VMS is not present, VMS won't run on 
|>it.

No - the difference is in the console firmware that is available.  The
"NT-only" systems lack the "SRM console" required by VMS and Digital UNIX.

|>Assuming the above is true, how does Digital UNIX fit into the picture?  
|>If a system can run Digital UNIX, can it also run VMS?  Or is there 
|>another variant which comes into play?

Assuming VMS has support for the system, yes.  I'm not aware of any systems
which support Digital UNIX but not VMS.

|>This all leads up to: I have heard that Digital UNIX will run on a 
|>Multia.  Will VMS?

No, but I have heard only rumors that people have Digital UNIX running on
a Multia.  Multia doesn't make a very good workstation - if you want something
cheap, look at the low end of the AlphaStation line (now under $5K) or a
system from one of the vendors of non-Digital Alpha-based systems.  Keep in
mind that it's not just the hardware, it's whether or not the OS "knows about"
the system board and its peripherals.
