phil@yrsk.demon.co.uk (Phil Ottewell (Yezerski Roper Ltd.)) wrote:
>I am thinking of adding a CD ROM jukebox to my VAXstation 4000 VLC at home.
>I asked a supplier about the Pioneer DRM624X, having seen it recommended on 
>comp.os.vms or comp.sys.dec, and was told that it would require 150 UKP worth
>of driver software (which they could supply) to make it work with VMS.
>
>My understanding was that the Pioneer was sub-lun addressable, and that all I
>needed to do was to use SYSGEN and CONNECT (say) DKA400, DKA401 etc., then
>define the DECW$BOOK logical, for example, as a search list so that all the
>disks would be automatically loaded and searched.
>
>Can anyone enlighten me on whether I really need 150 pounds worth of extra
>drivers, and which firm actually writes them ?

Well, I haven't tried the 624X yet, but we use a DRM604X here and are 
very happy with it.  It needs no extra drivers.  We use it with a 
VAXstation 4000-60.

All we do is SYSGEN CONNECT DKA401, DKA402, etc.  (DKA400 is auto 
configured.)

If you try to access another disk while the drive is changing disks, it 
tends to log errors.  However, these errors can be ignored (they are 
SCSI busy errors) as they don't seem to cause any problems.  The only 
driver software I could imagine is something which is able to suppress 
these errors.  I wouldn't bother with it.

Before we bought out 604X, I talked to some of the people at Pioneer 
responsible for its firmware.  They said they had worked with DEC to 
ensure that it would work properly with VAXstations and VMS and that it 
didn't need any special software.  You might check with Pioneer to see 
if the same is true of the 624X.  (Please let me know what you find 
out.)

