In article <314727CD.7D33@indirect.com>, "Joseph R. Jones" <jrjones@indirect.com> writes:

>I am looking to buy a used DEC 5000/120, and I was wondering if there 
>was any information about this machine available on-line.

There might be something on gatekeeper.dec.com.  

The DECsystem/DECstation 5000 Model 120 was the low end of the
2nd wave of 5000 models introduced.  Uses an R3000 as the CPU.
The CPU, cache and other essential glue are arranged on daughter
card.  Four different daughter cards (at four different CPU speeds)
were produced.  The motherboard held memory, built-in SCSI adapter,
Ethernet adapter, four line serial interface, etc.

It also had three 12.5 Mhz TURBOchannel slots.  The TURBOchannel was
a 32 bit bus with a nominal speed of 25 Mhz.  Running full out it was
a 100 MB/sec bus.  I've heard of tests that ran it up to 80 MB/sec under
real loads.  The half speed version of the Model 100s was to help reduce
the cost.  For some TURBOchannel devices it also helped *noticably*
reduc the performance, probably something not intended.

When a 5000 model came equiped with a graphics subsystem it was a
DECstation, without a DECsystem and intended as server.

The memory used by the Model 100 family was the same as used in
the DECstation 3100.  Introduced around the same were larger capacity
SIMMs of the same design allowing the Model 100 to support interesting
amount of memory.  If I recollect, the Model 100 supported up to 32 MB
using the original 3100 SIMM and 128 with the larger one.

The SCSI adapter supported DMA access from adapter into system
memory.  The previous adapters had used a shared buffer on the
adapter and required the host to copy between the two.  The
adapter is single-ended, narrow, 5 MB/sec SCSI.  It doesn't
support tagged command queueing, but does support synchronous
transfers.  It is interesting to note that the DMA design
actually reduced the cost of the adapter over the previous
design.

The four versions of daughter card available were the 20 Mhz R3000
Model 120, 25 Mhz Model 125, 33 Mhz Model 133 (see a pattern here?)
and an R4000 that was probably around 50 Mhz, thought I don't recall.

>
>Any URL's would be appriciated.

An interesting idea that...  One of these days I'll get around
to suggesting we start a hardware FAQ.

>
>Also, I have a few questions about the machine-
>
>      How does it compare performance-wise to a
>      pentium 75 running BSD? (assuming same ram)

Hurm...  20 Mhz R3000 vs. 75 Mhz Pentium.  Clock speed alone
makes the Pentium just over three times faster.  The Pentium
probably has other advantages in performance.  So, the DECstation
will be slower.

>      How well suited is this system to normal
>      (non-server) use?

If the price is right, it is a pretty decent system.  The Model 120
was the slowest daughter card produced.  Most supported versions of
ULTRIX are pretty memory hungry.  The half speed TURBOchannel helped
make the performance of some of the graphics adapters fairly poor
for terminal emulation.  All recommendations I saw were "get an HX",
to solve the problem.  Having been through it once, that did seem like
a reasonable soltuion.
 
