I have a Digital Personal DECstation 5000/33 with a defective
SERIAL.bus that I want to repair.
The SERIAL.bus is the keyboard, mouse, whatever interface
much like the ADB found on Apple MacIntosh.

The SERIAL.bus of the DECstation is not totaly dead: it is able
to driver either a mouse or a keyboard, but not both together.
Both mouse and keyboard function OK alone and in combination
when connected to a different DECstation. So i suspect the SERIAL.bus
drivers are damaged in some way. The damage was probably caused
by a broken mouse cable that had broken wires in the sheaths that
caused short circuits of the SERIAL.bus clock and/or data lines to
either ground or the +12V supply. I was able to repair the mouse
cable, the mouse is working fine when connected to a healthy DECstation.

I have already located the parts on the mainboard that are relevant
for the SERIAL.bus: a custom chip, 4 diodes, 2 transistors, 6 resistors
and a noise filter.

So my question: are there any documents describing the SERIAL.bus
drivers in detail or is there any one who can give me this information ?

I hope I can get the DECstations running again by replacing just
the defective parts of the defective driver (clock or data driver, or
even both).

BTW: I successfully repaired a different DECstation 5000/33s SERIAL.bus
that had a blown +12V supply by replaing a SMD device that looked like
a diode (probably a shottky type, because the voltage drop was well
below 0.6V as measured on a healthy DECstation).

  Thanks
	Joerg
