Subject: Re: Performance with LSM,HSZ40,Mirroring over 2 PCI controllers with Alpha 1000
Date: 1996/03/06
Message-ID: <4hj8np$6ug@dartvax.dartmouth.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 142069769
references: <313B3271.2C4E@charon.cvi.ns.nl>
to: nsi_fok@charon.cvi.ns.nl
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: ValleyNet
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.databases.oracle,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.osf.osf1
x-mailer: Mozilla 1.1N (Windows; I; 16bit)

The HSZ40 uses a fast-wide (FWD) SCSI channel to attach to the Alpha.  The max
throughput of this adapter (KZPSA I think) is about 14Mbyte/second.  It is therefore 
impractical to put two HSZ40's on the the same KZP, since such a configuration would 
be a severe bandwidth limitation. So, performance concerns indicate that each HSZ40 
have a dedicated SCSI-PCI adapter.  The 2100 series has a limitation of 3 PCI slots 
(I have heard but not confirmend that you can not configure more than 2 KZPSA's on 
the 2100).

Are you referring to the PCI RAID controller as an alternative to the HSZ40, or to 
the KZPSA, more correctly called an adapter?  The PCI RAID controller, OEM'ed from 
Mylex (KZPSC) should give you better performance.  But, the PCI controller limits 
your configuration options since the disks have to be within 1.5 meters of the 
controller, unless you incur considerable extra expense.  Each KZPSA has 3 SCSI 
buses, so you would need two to be equivalent to the 6 SCSI buses on the HSZ40.

I have done testing on the EISA version of the Mylex controller, and have found that 
more than 2 disks per bus doing large concurrent transfers causes severe 
bottlenecks.  I do not know how much better the PCI controller is, but I am sure it 
is faster, perhaps much.

For data warehousing, I do not recommend RAID: even RAID 0.  If you are using 
Parallel Query, you will do better by explicitly striping at the Oracle datafile 
level.  Also, in SMP configurations, AdvFs is a bottleneck, at least until DEC UNIX 
4.0 is available; all I/O is funneled through  CPU 0.

My experience is more with data warehouse applications, and I am not sure how 
relevant my information is in transaction processing setups.

Hope this helps!

Marty Himmelstein
marty.himmelstein@valley.net




