In article <4hn6p2$k9r@cauldron.spdc.ti.com>, dostalik@cauldron.spdc.ti.com (William Dostalik) writes:
> I've just begin shopping around for a workstation.  My primary concern
> is raw number crunching speed.  I don't care about fancy graphics.
> It needs to be able to run fortran code (for me) and C/C++ (for
> the infidel) as fast as it can.  I'm only interested in floating point
> calculation per $.
> 
> We already have several Sparc20's so I suspect there would be no problem 
> networking problems with the Ultra, what about the Alpha?

I have had no problems networking any Alpha running OpenVMS and Unix.  Just
last week at a Decus symposium I found myself in the position of installing
Digital Unix for the first time.  The last time I did any Unix work was about 3
years ago (Ultrix) and the last I did a Unix install was over 6 years
(Intergraph's Clipper Unix).  I'm normally a OpenVMS guy.  I had only a 20 page
or so installation guide for Unix, and I barely read it.

My Digital Unix install was absolutely smooth.  It was one of the smoothest
installations I've ever done, and Digital is to be commended.  The basic
install finished by bringing up a system configuration menu.  I selected all
the choices, and had TCP/IP up and running in no time flat.

On other Alpha systems that I've installed OpenVMS on, I've had no problems
with any networking.  We're running TCP/IP (from TGV), DECnet/OSI (which
includes DECnet and X.25), and AppleTalk.

At the Decus symposium, our Cafe Internet had Alphas running OpenVMS, Windows
NT, and OpenVMS.  We're just a bunch of volunteers, and had no problems getting
it all going (except that Netscape 2.0 was not available for NT on Alpha yet).

One the things Digital has always been really good at is networking.

> I'm tempted to got with the SUN just for safety, but the Alpha numbers
> and cost make it a very difficult choice.  Any comments?  

The Alpha should be a safe choice.  It's number are impressive, and for
number-crunching applications, I wouldn't hesitate to purchase one.  For our
own application mix, which is mostly transaction processing based, the verdict
is still out - we're happy with our Digital Vaxes and find they do the job for
us.  We do expect to migrate to the faster Alphas when they're released though.

One new product that really impressed us at the symposium was FX32!  This
allows for real-time executable translation of Intel code to Alpha code.  We
installed Netscape 2.0 for NT for Intel and it ran quite well.  A few bugs are
still lingering, but it's in internal field test and is scheduled for general
availability this summer - I'm confident that the bugs will be ironed out by
then.

