  If that's a direct quote, I'd be interested in receiving some offline
  e-mail containing that particular manager's name, and I'll check with
  the manager and see if this was the intended interpretation of that
  particular comment.

  As for contracting for maintenance service from Digital (and from
  various other service vendors), there are typically varying levels
  of service available (at least from Digital), ranging from on-site
  hot spares and on-site service technicians, to providing mail-in
  swaps and technical support (DECmailer: Digital Assisted Services)
  for systems maintained by technically-minded end-users.  What sorts
  of services is required at a particular site is highly dependent on
  the time-critical nature of the systems, and (obviously) how much
  one is willing to spend -- and the required service levels can and
  often do vary across different systems at the same customer site.

  I have regularly dealt with systems that suffice nicely with service
  turnarounds measured in days or weeks, to systems that have hot-standby
  systems, on-site spares, redundent hardware, etc., and require rapid
  maintenance turnarounds. 

  (Also being fairly familiar with 911 services -- that's the typical
  emergency telephone number in North America -- and emergency services
  communications, I would expect such a site to have a seperate hot-backup
  site available at all times, I'd expect dedicated seperately-routed
  communications links, shadowing of all disk data, off-site or swapping
  storage of backups, and I would expect these services to have a fairly
  high-tier maintenance contract(s) in place for any necessary repairs.
  As I'm sure you're well aware, staffing, configuring, and running a busy
  emergency services communications center is far harder than it looks.)

  What sort of contract is in place at the site you are referencing?

