> Amongst my collection of outdated equipment sits a PDT-11 (yes, that is a T).
> It has two 8" floppies, parallel, console and eight serial ports. I have docs
> for RT11 and a word processing application that seems to like to output to
> daisy-wheel printers

That's a PDT-11/150 (Programmable Data Terminal).  The PDT-11/130 had TU58s,
better known as DECtape IIs.  Linear "Floppy disk", very slow.

> After browsing the documentatio (a long time ago), I learned that this unit
> could even be configured for a multi-tasking kernel - provided you had enough
> memory, which I don't.
>
> Is there anybody out there who knows something about the PDT-11? What are you
> doing with yours (now or then)? Does anybody have memory for this thing, or
> even know what memory it uses and where to get it?

These were "bounded bus" machines - not expandable.  The LSI-11 CPU could only
address 64K bytes - 56K of Ram and 8 Kb of I/O space.  (Thinks: might be
60K RAM and 4K I/O).  They typically ran RT-11 with multiterminal support and 
Multiuser BASIC.

I was given one.  I ran it once to prove it worked then put it in storage.
I have lots of floppy disks that came with it but I haven't scanned them all.

> -- Peter
> P.S. I've just got to figure that any general computing device with all those
> ports has *got* to be able to do *something* useful! :)

