Andrew Brownsword wrote:
> What is far more interesting is that the single processor UltraSparc did
> so well.  Clearly its v9 multimedia instruction set made big difference.
> And what was the UltraSparc's clock rate?  Not some super-high Alpha-like
> speed, I'm sure.

This really a totally bogus comment. You can't compare different CPU
srchitectures with each other by commenting on their performance at a
special clock rate. Different processors has different design goals.

The basic directions is either (1) to do as much as possible in each
clock cycle or (2) to make the clock cycels go as fast as possible.

Sparc and Alpha is almost as far as part as you can get. They are
both extremes on each end of the scale. Sparc is designed to do a lot
in each cycle, but it is really very hard to scale up in clock rates.
Alpha doesn't do too much in one clock cycle, but is designed to sacle
very well to extremly high clock rates (Digital say they will pass 1GHz
with the ALphas before 2000).

I don't say wich way who is the most efficient one, it still remains to
be seen. Only the future will tell.

