=yesterday. My immediate reaction was to panic, but on consideration I rebooted 
=the nodes instead. They came up fine with new licenses in place. I think the 
=problem occurs because people have installed the new CSLG PAKs and not 
=rebooted. The fact that the old CSLG PAKs happened to have an expiration date  
=of Feb 29 just added to the Leap Year paranoia...

The PAK installation procedure, for each license:
	1)  Disables the license
	2)  Registers the new license
	3)  Unloads the old license
	4)  Enables the license
	5)  Loads the new license
on the node on which the installation is done. Now, if you've got a shared
system disk (and don't have separate license databases for the various nodes),
then this will put the new licenses in the license database for all nodes, but
will only load the new licenses on the node on which the installation procedure
was run.  Rebooting the remaining nodes in your cluster WILL load the new
licenses, but it's a bit drastic.  A simpler solution would be to search the
procedure that installed the new PAKs for the string "license unload,"
outputting the results to a command file, then execute that command file
followed by the command "License load."
