wrath@cs.umbc.edu (Vijay Gill) wrote:
>
>I have gotten two old VAXes to work.
>
>One is a DEC VAXStation 3100 with a graphics head that can run
>decw-motif.  However, the monitor is needed elsewhere.  Anyone have
>any pin outs etc on how to make the VAXStation 3100 talk with a vt220.
>
>I also have salavaged a VAXStation 3200, and it too has a graphics
>head running decwindows.  I would like to replace that with a vt220 as
>well.
>
>I have looked at the microvax home page/FAQ at
>http://www.csc.liv.ac.uk/users/edward/mvax_faq.html
>and it this is the relevant part:
>
>1. FAQ. How do I set up a system console on a mVAX 3100?
>   Ans: There is a little switch on the back - you must reach it with
>   a screwdriver. Push it UP then connect a VT to the printer port.
>
>1) Is this the same procedure for a VAXSTation?  I have read else
>where that you need to disable the graphics head before it will
>default to the printer port and 2) what sort of cable will fit the
>printer port on the back of a VAXStation 3100 and 3200?
>
>I have looked at the FAQ and at my archives for this newsgroup, but I
>didn't seem to find anything relevant.

For the VAXstation 3100, all you need to do is flip the switch.  On my 
system, VMS then identifies the system as a VAXserver 3100, so the 
VAXstation PAKs no longer work.  If you put an MMJ adapter on the VT220 
(sorry, I don't know the part number), you can use an MMJ cable to hook 
it up. NOTE: The graphics head is still present and active.

For the VAXstation 3200, you can (1) remove the graphics controller.  
The port on the CPU cabinet kit then becomes the console.  The machine 
will probably become a VAX 3500 for licensing purposes. or (2) connect a 
terminal to the CPU cabinet kit and hit break each time you boot it.  (I 
have been told this works, but have never tried it myself).  The boot 
ROMs will then use the terminal as the console.  I don't know how VMS 
will ID this configuration.

Good luck!

