In article <3137A524.BC2@charon.cvi.ns.nl>, Hans Fokker <nsi_fok@charon.cvi.ns.nl> writes:

>We are thinking of buying a second Storage Works Cabinet with it own 
>HSZ40 Controller.
>
>The Idea is this :
>
>2 * PCI Controllers in the Alpha 1000
>2 * Storage Works Cabinet's
>2 * HSZ40's (Disks configured -=> Stripe sets.)
>OSF/1 3.2
>LSM as the software to mirror the disks behind the HSZ40's.
>This machine is running as a database server for SAP v.2.2e 
>
>B:) Is this the best configuration for possible for this function. 
>	(as a database server)

This configuration has the advantage that is an adapter or
HSZ40 dies all the data will still be available through
the other controller/adapter.  The disadvantage is that
host based mirror puts some load on the CPU.

An alternative is to use mirroring on the HSZ40, which
offloads the mirroring to the HSZ40.  Unfortunately,
this makes the adapter and HSZ40 potential failure
points.  A 3rd and 4th HSZ40 can fix this by using
reduandant HSZ40s and they will handle failover if
one fails, but you still have the potential problem
of the adapter being a failure point.

Another advantage of mirroring on the HSZ40 is that it
supports automatic sparing.  You can specify a set of
drives to be used as spares and if one fails, a replacement
will be selected and brought up to date.  I don't think
LSM has this feature.

If you need higher bandwidth performance (and possible through-put
performance) you can switch the striping and mirroring.  Mirror
at the HSZ40 and stripe between them.

It is all a matter of balancing between cost, availability and
performance.

>Erik Bais
